Under CAR 401.19, a student pilot permit holder may act as pilot-in-command on a daytime VFR training flight in Canada with no passengers on board.
The holder of a student pilot permit may act as pilot-in-command of an aircraft of the category for which the permit is endorsed if:
* the flight is conducted for the purpose of the holder’s flight training;
* the flight is conducted in Canada;
* the flight is conducted under day VFR;
* the flight is conducted under the direction and supervision of a person qualified to provide training toward the permit, licence or rating for which the pilot-in-command experience is required; and
* no passenger is carried on board.
Note: The holder of a student pilot permit who is enrolled in an integrated course may act as pilot-in-command during a flight that is conducted under day or night VFR.

The question asks if the holder of a multi-crew pilot licence can act as pilot-in-command of any aircraft for their own flight training. 

According to section 401.32 of Canadian Aviation Regulations, paragraph (a), a multi-crew pilot licence holder may act as co-pilot of a turbine-powered aeroplane that is operated day or night, under VFR, VFR OTT or IFR, if the aeroplane is a transport category aircraft; 
paragraph (b) specifies that it must be an aeroplane for which a minimum flight crew document has been issued that specifies a minimum flight crew of two pilots;
paragraph (c) specifies that it must be of a type in respect of which the holder’s licence is endorsed with a rating.
Section 401.19 of Canadian Aviation Regulations states that the holder of a multi-crew pilot licence may act as pilot-in-command of any aircraft for the purpose of the holder’s flight training if the flight training is conducted in accordance with section 401.19.

Therefore, based on these sections, it appears that a multi-crew pilot licence holder can act as pilot-in-command of an aeroplane for their own flight training under certain conditions.

For a powered aircraft, a take-off, approach, or landing can be conducted within a built-up area of a city or town at a place that is not an airport, heliport, or military aerodrome if:
- The place is not set apart for the operation of aircraft;
- The flight is conducted without creating a hazard to persons or property on the surface;
- The aircraft is operated
  (i) for the purpose of a police operation that is conducted in the service of a police authority, 
  or 
(ii) for the purpose of saving human life.
- Permission to use the place as a launch site has been obtained from the land owner;
- A special aviation event is not being held at that place at the time of take-off;
- No written objection in respect of the use of the place as a launch site has been received by the Minister from a competent land use authority;
- The diameter of the launch site is no less than the greater of 
  (i) 100 feet, and 
  (ii) the greatest dimension of the balloon, be it the length, width or height, plus 25 per cent; 
- The take-off point within the launch site is upwind of the highest obstacle in the take-off path by a horizontal distance equal to the height of that obstacle, and the take-off is conducted
  (ii) where the flight path of the balloon is directly over residential or commercial buildings or over an open-air assembly of persons, using the maximum rate of climb possible, considering operational and passenger safety.
- The landing is necessary to avoid endangering the safety of the persons on board;
- The pilot-in-command contacts the appropriate air traffic services unit, either prior to landing or as soon as possible after landing, and provides 
  (i) the balloon’s nationality mark and registration mark,
  (ii) the estimated or actual, as applicable, time and location of the landing, and
  (iii) the reasons why it is believed that the safety of the persons on board is or was endangered.

Note: The above answer is based on sections 602.13 and 702.22 of Canadian Aviation Regulations, Part VI and Subpart 2, Division I, General Operating and Flight Rules, and Division III, Aerial Work, respectively.
